.TITLE "git"

.DIV "DESCRIPTION"
Git is a version control system used to **track changes** in your code.

It was created by __Linus Torvalds__ in __2005__.
You can think of it like a timeline for your project. 
Instead of saving separate copies of files, you save checkpoints on a single line.

.DIV "EXAMPLE"
git status

.DIV "EXPLANATION"
**git status** shows the current state of your project.

Here, __git__ is the tool you are using.
__status__ is the subcommand that checks for modified files.

This helps you **see what has changed** before you decide to save anything.

.DIV "EXAMPLE"
git add file.txt

.DIV "EXPLANATION"
**git add** prepares your changes to be saved.

Here, __git__ is the tool.
__add__ is the action of moving files to the staging area.
__file.txt__ is the specific file you want to include.

You can **add a single file**, or use **git add .** to **include everything** in the current folder.
This is like putting items into a box before you tape it shut.

.DIV "EXAMPLE"
git commit -m "update project"

.DIV "EXPLANATION"
**git commit** records your changes permanently.

Here, __git__ is the tool.
__commit__ is the action that saves the checkpoint.
__-m__ is a flag used to add a message.
"update project" is the description of your work.

This creates a permanent entry in your project's history.

.DIV "SUMMARY"
Git organizes your project history so you never lose your work.
Get into the habit of **running git status** often to **understand your timeline** before you **save changes**.
